  "statement": "For the curve $y=x^{3}-3x$, find the tangent at $x=1$, show it meets the curve again at $(-2,-2)$, and find the area enclosed between the tangent and the curve.",
  "solution": "At $x=1$, $y=-2$ and $y'=3x^{2}-3=0$, so the tangent is $y=-2$. Meeting the curve: $x^{3}-3x=-2\\Rightarrow x^{3}-3x+2=0=(x-1)^{2}(x+2)$, so $x=-2$, $y=-2$. On $[-2,1]$, $x^{3}-3x+2\\ge 0$, so area $=\\int_{-2}^{1}(x^{3}-3x+2)\\,dx =\\left[\\tfrac14x^{4}-\\tfrac32x^{2}+2x\\right]_{-2}^{1}=\\tfrac34-(-6)=\\tfrac{27}{4}$.",
  "hints": [
    "Differentiate to get the gradient at $x=1$.",
    "Solve $x^{3}-3x=-2$ for the second intersection.",
    "Integrate the vertical gap between curve and tangent."
  ],
